  <page>
    <title>Stillwater, Minnesota</title>
    <id>122064</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41634717</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-28T18:02:21Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>68.165.42.120</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Stillwater.jpg|thumb|right|230px|A Stillwater city limit sign]]

'''Stillwater''' is a city located in [[Washington County, Minnesota|Washington County]], [[Minnesota]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 15,143.  It is the [[county seat]] of [[Washington County, Minnesota|Washington County]][[Geographic references|&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt;]].
Stillwater is part of the [[Minneapolis-Saint Paul|Twin City Metro Area]].

==Geography==
[[Image:MNMap-doton-Stillwater.png|right|Location of Stillwater, Minnesota]]
According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 18.9 [[square kilometer|km²]] (7.3 [[square mile|mi²]]).  16.8 km² (6.5 mi²) of it is land and 2.1 km² (0.8 mi²) of it is water.  The total area is 11.37% water.

Stillwater receives an average snowfall of 42&quot;. Average rainfall is 24&quot;. Each year has an average of 14 days above 90 degrees Fahrenheit.

==Demographics==
As of the [[census]][[Geographic references#2|&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]] of [[2000]], there are 15,143 people, 5,797 households, and 4,115 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 903.7/km² (2,340.0/mi²).  There are 5,926 housing units at an average density of 353.6/km² (915.7/mi²).  The racial makeup of the city is 97.52% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 0.32% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.28% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.57% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.02% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.36% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 0.93% from two or more races.  0.98% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 5,797 households out of which 36.8% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 57.2% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 10.8% have a female householder with no husband present, and 29.0% are non-families. 24.3% of all households are made up of individuals and 9.0% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.55 and the average family size is 3.07.

In the city the population is spread out with 27.7% under the age of 18, 6.3% from 18 to 24, 28.8% from 25 to 44, 25.4% from 45 to 64, and 11.8% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 38 years.  For every 100 females there are 92.9 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 86.7 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $57,154, and the median income for a family is $72,188. Males have a median income of $49,158 versus $33,680 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $27,163.  4.3% of the population and 3.0% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 5.6% of those under the age of 18 and 5.3% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

==History==
On [[July 29]] and [[September 29]], [[1837]], treaties were signed between the [[Federal government of the United States|US government]] and the local [[Ojibwa]] and [[Sioux|Dakota]] nations that allowed settlement in the St. Croix Valley. In 1848, the town was founded in earnest by settlers drawn by the area's then-abundant lumber and river traffic, making it one of Minnesota's oldest towns, predating Minneapolis by several years.

Lumber mills were started on the [[St. Croix River (Wisconsin-Minnesota)|St. Croix River]], and for many years logs were sent down the St. Croix and through Stillwater. [[Steamboat]]s were used most widely from [[1860]]–[[1890]], and a few are still used for entertainment purposes today.  During the 20th century, the town diminished in importance as the region's lumber became scarce.  It has since become a residential suburb of the Twin Cities.

==Attractions==
* [[Arcola Mills]] [http://www.arcolamills.org/]
* [[Isaac Staples' Mill]]
* [[Käthe Wohlfahrt]] (Christmas shop) [http://www.wohlfahrt.com/]
* [[Loome Antiquarian and Theological Booksellers]] [http://www.loomebooks.com/]
* [[Lowell Inn]] [http://www.lowellinn.com/]
* [[Lumberjack Days]] [http://www.lumberjackdays.com/]
* [[Minnesota Zephyr]] [http://www.minnesotazephyr.com/]
* [[St. Croix Boom Site]]
* [[St. Croix Valley Recreation Center]]
* [[Stillwater Bridge|Stillwater Lift Bridge]] [http://www.dot.state.mn.us/metro/projects/liftbridge/index.html]
* [[Stillwater Trolley]] [http://www.stillwatertrolley.com/]

==Famous people from Stillwater==

* [[Celeste (porn star)|Celeste]]
* [[Sky Lopez]]

==External links==
*[http://www.ci.stillwater.mn.us/ City of Stillwater, Minnesota]
*[http://www.mystillwater.com/ MyStillwater.com]
*[http://www.stillwater.k12.mn.us/834/ Stillwater Area Schools]
*[http://www.stillwatertraveler.com/ Stillwater Tourism Site]
*[http://www.wchsmn.org/ Washington County Historical Society]

{{Minnesota}}

[[Category:Washington County, Minnesota]]
[[Category:Cities in Minnesota]]

[[io:Stillwater, Minnesota]]</text>
    </revision>
