  <page>
    <title>St. James, Minnesota</title>
    <id>122085</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34157935</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-06T21:14:13Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Jcbarr</username>
        <id>482173</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>disambiguation link repair ([[Wikipedia:Disambiguation pages with links|You can help!]])</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''St. James''' is a city located in [[Watonwan County, Minnesota]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 4,695.  It is the [[county seat]] of [[Watonwan County, Minnesota|Watonwan County]][[Geographic references|&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt;]]. 

== History ==
In 1870, the directors of the St. Paul and Sioux City railroads chose the future townsite of St. James as the midpoint for a new railroad linking the two cities. Named by railroad director and future Minnesota Gov. [[Henry Hastings Sibley]] and railroad president E.F. Drake, the new city was incorporated in [[April]] [[1871]]. It was the hometown of Minnesota Gov. [[Winfield Scott Hammond]], a [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] who served in [[1915]]. In the 1940s, Theodore S. Mondale, the father of future [[Vice President of the United States]] [[Walter Mondale]], was the pastor at First Methodist Church of St. James. Tony Downs Foods Co. has been a major employer since 1947, processing poultry and frozen meals.

== Geography ==
[[Image:MNMap-doton-St._James.png|right|Location of St. James, Minnesota]]
According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 6.0 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (2.3 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  5.9 km&amp;sup2; (2.3 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.1 km&amp;sup2; (0.04 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 0.87% water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]][[Geographic references#2|&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]] of [[2000]], there are 4,695 people, 1,845 households, and 1,186 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 791.6/km&amp;sup2; (2,050.3/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 2,006 housing units at an average density of 338.2/km&amp;sup2; (876.0/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 83.24% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 0.40% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.15% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.51% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.00% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 14.27% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 1.43% from two or more races.  23.77% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 1,845 households out of which 32.1% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 51.1% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 9.6% have a female householder with no husband present, and 35.7% are non-families. 32.0% of all households are made up of individuals and 17.7% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.50 and the average family size is 3.16.

In the city the population is spread out with 27.9% under the age of 18, 9.5% from 18 to 24, 23.9% from 25 to 44, 20.0% from 45 to 64, and 18.7% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 35 years.  For every 100 females there are 88.9 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 86.4 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $33,196, and the median income for a family is $40,993. Males have a median income of $30,036 versus $19,391 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $15,336.  10.8% of the population and 9.0% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 16.5% of those under the age of 18 and 9.9% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

==External links==

*[http://stjames.govoffice.com St. James City Government Website]
*[http://www.stjamesmn.org St. James Chamber of Commerce Website]

[[Category:Watonwan County, Minnesota]]
[[Category:Cities in Minnesota]]

[[io:St. James, Minnesota]]</text>
    </revision>
