      <id>40399380</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-20T07:23:45Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>24.164.208.24</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">:''For the Italian explorer of Egyptian antiquities., see [[Giovanni Battista Belzoni]].''

'''Belzoni''' is a city located in [[Humphreys County, Mississippi]], in the [[Mississippi Delta]] region.  As of the 2000 census, the city had a total population of 2,663.  It is the [[county seat]] of [[Humphreys County, Mississippi|Humphreys County]]{{GR|6}}. It was named for the 19th century Italian [[archaeologist]]/[[explorer]] [[Giovanni Battista Belzoni]]. 

The area was named Farm-Raised Catfish Capital of the World in 1976 by then Governor [[Cliff Finch]], since it produces more farm-raised [[catfish]] than any other U.S. county. 40,000 acres (162 km&amp;sup2;) of the county are underwater, used to grow [[catfish]]. About 60 % of U.S. farm-raised [[catfish]] are grown within a 65 mile (100 km) radius of Belzoni. However, the title &quot;Catfish Capital&quot; has also been claimed by [[Savannah, Tennessee]], and [[Des Allemands, Louisiana]].

== Geography ==
Belzoni is located at 33&amp;deg;10'53&quot; North, 90&amp;deg;29'8&quot; West (33.181324, -90.485663){{GR|1}}.

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 2.5 [[square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]] (1.0 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  2.5 km&amp;sup2; (1.0 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and none of it is covered by water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 2,663 people, 934 households, and 640 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 1,071.0/km&amp;sup2; (2,777.8/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 1,018 housing units at an average density of 409.4/km&amp;sup2; (1,061.9/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 30.49% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 68.08% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.04% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.64% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.00% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.60% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 0.15% from two or more races.  1.35% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 934 households out of which 33.8% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 35.3% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 28.6% have a female householder with no husband present, and 31.4% are non-families. 28.2% of all households are made up of individuals and 11.8% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.83 and the average family size is 3.49.

In the city the population is spread out with 32.2% under the age of 18, 11.4% from 18 to 24, 27.3% from 25 to 44, 17.8% from 45 to 64, and 11.3% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 30 years.  For every 100 females there are 85.7 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 77.5 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $20,690, and the median income for a family is $25,521. Males have a median income of $26,466 versus $15,833 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $13,022.  35.0% of the population and 29.3% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 47.4% of those under the age of 18 and 27.3% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.  It is not an awesome place.

==Semi=Famous residents==
*[[Pinetop Perkins]], blues musician (b. 1913)
Boog Lloyd, b. 1981

== External links ==
