  <page>
    <title>Picayune, Mississippi</title>
    <id>122391</id>
    <revision>
      <id>37014115</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-27T23:51:42Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>131.95.170.155</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Picayune''' is a city located in [[Pearl River County, Mississippi|Pearl River County]] in [[Mississippi]], a [[U.S. state|state]] of the [[United States|United States of America]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 10,535. Picayune received considerable damage during Hurricane Katrina, and since the storm, its location an hour north of both New Orleans and the Mississippi Gulf Coast has caused its population to surge with evacuees.

== Geography ==
Picayune is located at 30&amp;deg;31'41&quot; North, 89&amp;deg;40'49&quot; West (30.528089, -89.680182){{GR|1}}.

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 30.6 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (11.8 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  30.5 km&amp;sup2; (11.8 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.1 km&amp;sup2; (0.04 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 0.34% water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 10,535 people, 4,100 households, and 2,865 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 345.9/km&amp;sup2; (895.6/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 4,568 housing units at an average density of 150.0/km&amp;sup2; (388.3/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 62.02% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 35.92% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.38% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.30% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.05% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.18% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 1.15% from two or more races.  1.15% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 4,100 households out of which 31.8% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 45.0% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 20.8% have a female householder with no husband present, and 30.1% are non-families. 26.4% of all households are made up of individuals and 11.8% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.54 and the average family size is 3.06.

In the city the population is spread out with 27.0% under the age of 18, 9.4% from 18 to 24, 25.5% from 25 to 44, 23.0% from 45 to 64, and 15.1% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 36 years.  For every 100 females there are 82.8 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 79.2 males.

The [[median]] income for a household in the city is $26,958, and the median income for a family is $33,260. Males have a median income of $31,438 versus $20,035 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $15,798.  20.8% of the population and 18.9% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 28.2% of those under the age of 18 and 17.6% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

== Transportation ==

[[Amtrak]]'s [[Crescent (Amtrak)|Crescent train]] connects Picayune with the cities of [[New York City|New York]], [[Philadelphia, Pennsylvania|Philadelphia]], [[Baltimore, Maryland|Baltimore]], [[Washington, D.C.|Washington]], [[Charlotte, North Carolina|Charlotte]], [[Atlanta, Georgia|Atlanta]], [[Birmingham, Alabama|Birmingham]] and [[New Orleans, Louisiana|New Orleans]]. The Amtrak station is situated at 100 South Highway 11.

Interstate 59 connects Picayune to New Orleans LA in the south and Hattiesburg MS, Meridian MS and Birmingham Al, to the North.

Picayune Municipal Airport [http://www.airnav.com/airport/KMJD (KMJD)] has a 5000 ft runway and is a popular destination for private fixed wing and rotary aircraft visiting the New Orleans area.  Rental car, Taxi and Limousine services are available,

== History ==
Picayune was founded in 1904, named by [[Elia Jane Poitevent]] Nicholson, the owner and the publisher of the ''[[New Orleans Times-Picayune]]''.

== Points of interest ==
* [[Crosby Arboretum]]

== External links ==
